  "story": {
    "bet": [
      {
        "text": "Do less better: master the simple things, with one pivot that shoots, amps and shoots over a defender.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s20"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "demand": [
      {
        "text": "Score in the speaker from all reasonable distances and in the amp, and pick up notes anywhere on the open field. The trap was ignored: designing for it compromised the whole robot, its points were not worth it in eliminations, and other scoring reached the same ranking point.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s3",
          "s20"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "auto": [
      {
        "text": "Drive-to-point paths. If the Limelight sees a note, the chassis shifts to take it; if the note is missing, the robot diverts to the next. Speaker tag angle and distance correct its position.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s3"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "cycle": [
      {
        "text": "The under-bumper intake takes the note and hands it to the shooter; the arm tracks the speaker and LEDs turn blue when the shot will go in. For the amp and passing, the chassis snaps to a heading. Human players stand at both source and amp.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s3",
          "s19"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "endgame": [
      {
        "text": "Drive into the chain late with hooks up, lining up about five seconds before the end with a partner; when power is lost at the buzzer the cylinders retract and lift the robot. The center hook lets it hang with a partner, and the team says late climbs earned a few ranking points.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s3",
          "s6",
          "s19"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "fallback": [
      {
        "text": "The arm can be raised at set positions to shoot over a tall defender. The tank holds air for two hang attempts with the compressor off. The driver can let go of heading snap, or rehome the arm, by hand.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s19",
          "s6",
          "s13"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "gave_up": [
      {
        "text": "No trap. Flywheels stay at one speed, since slowing them up close hurt autos that shoot while driving; some close shots go in and out.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s20",
          "s12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "On Einstein, a post says, both losses were close, a partner had trouble with shuttled notes and an opponent stole them. Another wonders whether it should have cycled rather than passed.",
        "basis": "observer",
        "src": [
          "s23",
          "s24"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "changed": [
      {
        "text": "After the first event the flywheels were tipped down 6 degrees, because the arm could not go low enough for wing line shots. Aluminum intake side plates replaced polycarbonate, ribs stiffened the rear frame, and a second hook was added.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s3",
          "s7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Looking back, the intake should probably have used sushi rollers.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s11"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "outcome": [
      {
        "text": "67-3-0 in official play; won the Galileo division; captain of the Galileo alliance on Einstein; ranked 50 by championship OPR (47.07, Galileo).",
        "basis": "record",
        "src": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "functions": [
    {
